---
import GuideLayout from '../../components/GuideLayout.astro';

const title = 'How to Set Up 2FA on PayPal (TOTP Authenticator)';

const description =
  'Enable authenticator-app two-factor authentication on PayPal and generate live, browser-based TOTP codes — no mobile app or SMS code required.';

---

<GuideLayout
  serviceName="PayPal"
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/guides/paypal/"
  dateModified="2026-11-23"
  datePublished="2026-11-10"
  lastReviewed="2026-11-23"
  reviewedPrefix="UI wording and navigation"
  reviewedClaim="unverified — paypal.com answered a real browser with HTTP 403 and an empty page on 23 November 2026, so no PayPal help article could be read"
  sources={[
    {
      label: 'RFC 6238 — TOTP: Time-Based One-Time Password Algorithm',
      href: 'https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6238',
      kind: 'independent',
    },
  ]}
  intro="PayPal’s 2-step verification is worth turning on whichever methods it offers. Where an authenticator-app option appears, choose it: a phone number can be swapped by someone else, and money moves through this account. The part we could not check today is the exact wording — PayPal’s help centre returned HTTP 403 with an empty page to a real browser — so this page describes the control to look for rather than quoting a path."
  minutes={4}
  params={['6 digits', '30s period', 'SHA-1']}
  ctaTitle="Your PayPal account is a payment rail — treat the backup like one"
  ctaBody="PayPal can sit behind a password alone, and a stolen login moves real money. Save the setup secret and any recovery codes in an encrypted password manager, and review which 2-step methods stay enrolled once a quarter — a weaker fallback left on the account is the door you forgot about."
  deepDive={{
    heading: 'What this page can and cannot confirm',
    blocks: [
      {
        title: 'The TOTP mechanics are standards; the screen labels are not',
        body: 'RFC 6238 defines the 6-digit, 30-second code that any authenticator computes from the same secret, so the generator side of this guide is arithmetic rather than a vendor claim. PayPal’s own screens are the unverified half: on 23 November 2026 the site returned HTTP 403 with an empty body to a real browser, on the home page as well as the help centre, so no PayPal article or settings path could be read. Where this page names a control, treat it as the thing to look for, not as a label PayPal is known to print.',
      },
      {
        title: 'Why the authenticator option is worth preferring',
        body: 'An SMS code proves control of a phone number. Number porting, carrier social engineering and recycled SIMs all attack that proof, and the account holder usually finds out after the fact. A TOTP secret proves possession of a key that cannot be transferred without someone reading it. On an account that can send money, that difference is the whole argument.',
      },
      {
        title: 'Save what the screen gives you',
        body: 'If the setup flow prints a Base32 secret, keep it — it regenerates codes on any device, offline. If it offers recovery codes, download them at the same moment; they are the route back in when the phone is gone. Neither is worth reconstructing later from memory, and PayPal’s account recovery is slower than either.',
      },
    ],
  }}
  steps={[
    {
      title: 'Open PayPal security settings',
      body: 'Sign in at paypal.com, then open Settings (the gear icon) and look for “Security”. PayPal has moved this entry between profile and account settings over time, so find the “2-step verification” section rather than trusting a fixed route.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Start 2-step verification setup',
      body: 'Choose the setup control beside 2-step verification. If your screen offers SMS and an authenticator app, take the authenticator app — it is the TOTP factor, independent of your phone number. If only SMS is offered, the app option may be regional or account-dependent.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Reveal the secret',
      body: 'A QR code is the common setup screen. If a Base32 “secret key” is printed or linked for manual entry, copy that text; otherwise screenshot the QR and paste the image straight into the generator page — it is decoded on your device, never uploaded.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Generate, verify, activate',
      body: 'Paste the key or screenshot into 2facounter.com, copy the live code, and paste it back into PayPal’s confirmation field. 2-step verification flips to active and PayPal starts asking for a code at sign-in.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Audit your methods',
      body: 'Back on the 2-step verification screen, check every enrolled method. If SMS is still listed as a second method, decide deliberately whether to keep it — it is a weaker fallback. If PayPal offers recovery codes, download them now and store them away from the phone.',
    },
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: 'Does PayPal require 2FA?',
      a: 'This page cannot confirm PayPal’s current enforcement policy — that answer lives in the help centre we could not read. What is not in doubt is the value: a payment account guarded by a password alone is one credential leak away from a transfer, and 2-step verification is the cheapest fix available.',
    },
    {
      q: 'SMS codes vs authenticator app on PayPal — which should I pick?',
      a: 'If both are offered, the authenticator app. SMS codes die with your SIM: a SIM-swap attacker who ports your phone number receives your codes. TOTP lives on the device (or in this browser) and never transmits anything.',
    },
    {
      q: 'I lost my phone and can’t get PayPal codes — what now?',
      a: 'If you saved recovery codes, use one at sign-in. If SMS is still enrolled as a method and you kept the number, that still works. Without either, PayPal’s account recovery is the remaining route — we could not read its help centre today, so treat the exact steps as unknown rather than as described on this page.',
    },
    {
      q: 'PayPal rejects my code right after setup — why?',
      a: 'Most rejections are clock drift: the server and your device disagree on what "now" is, and the code rolls over mid-verification. Sync your system clock automatically, then submit the freshly displayed code. If a code mix-up ever locks you out, recovery exists — but a synced clock prevents the problem in the first place.',
    },
  ]}
/>
